Article 1 Where a shareholder individually or jointly holding 10 percent or more of the voting rights of all shareholders of a company initiates an action for dissolution of the company on any of the following grounds, and the action complies with the provisions of Article 182 of the Company Law, the People's Court shall accept the case:
(1) the company has been unable to convene a shareholders' meeting or a shareholders' general meeting for two years or more consecutively, and serious difficulties have arisen in the company's operation and management;
(2) when shareholders vote, the proportion required by law or the company's articles of association cannot be reached, and no effective resolution of the shareholders' meeting or the shareholders' general meeting can be adopted for two years or more consecutively, and serious difficulties have arisen in the company's operation and management;
(3) there are long-standing conflicts among the company's directors, which cannot be resolved through the shareholders' meeting or the shareholders' general meeting, and serious difficulties have arisen in the company's operation and management;
(4) other serious difficulties occur in operation and management, and the continued existence of the company would cause major losses to the interests of shareholders.
Where a shareholder initiates an action for dissolution of the company on the grounds that rights and interests such as the right to information and the right to claim profit distribution have been damaged, or that the company has suffered losses, or its property is insufficient to repay all debts, or that the company's business license of enterprise legal person has been revoked without liquidation, the People's Court shall not accept the case.

Article 7 A company shall, in accordance with the provisions of Article 70 of the Civil Code and Article 183 of the Company Law, establish a liquidation group within fifteen days from the date on which the cause for dissolution arises and commence liquidation on its own.
In any of the following circumstances, where creditors, shareholders, directors, or other interested parties apply to the People's Court to designate a liquidation group to carry out liquidation, the People's Court shall accept the application:
(1) after the time limit for dissolution expires, the company fails to establish a liquidation group to carry out liquidation;
(2) although a liquidation group has been established, it intentionally delays the liquidation;
(3) illegal liquidation may seriously damage the interests of creditors or shareholders.

Article 8 After accepting a company liquidation case, the People's Court shall promptly designate relevant persons to form a liquidation group.
Members of the liquidation group may be selected from the following persons or institutions:
(1) shareholders, directors, supervisors, and senior officers of the company;
(2) social intermediary institutions established according to law, such as law firms, accounting firms, and bankruptcy liquidation firms;
(3) persons who possess relevant professional knowledge and have obtained practice qualifications in social intermediary institutions established according to law, such as law firms, accounting firms, and bankruptcy liquidation firms.

Article 9 Where a member of the liquidation group designated by the People's Court falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may, upon application by creditors, shareholders, directors, or other interested parties, or ex officio, replace the member:
(1) the member has committed acts violating laws or administrative regulations;
(2) the member has lost professional competence or capacity for civil conduct;
(3) the member has committed acts that seriously damage the interests of the company or creditors.
